Define an explicit locked/imported/reviewed/signed session model with checked Soma expectations: signing requires a key, a parsed PSBT, and fresh explicit approval; locking invalidates approval and the active key reference.
Build the offline wallet UI with Reason-style Soma JSX, semantic HTML and responsive styling: locked wallet, key-import/unlock flow, PSBT import, full transaction review, explicit approval, signed-PSBT export, and lock/reset. Do not claim unavailable backend actions work.
Extend the wallet-local libsecp256k1 WASM bridge to accept runtime-supplied private keys and digests, derive compressed public keys and sign deterministically, with typed Soma exports, known vectors, malformed-key rejection and independent signature verification. No secret values may be constant-folded or logged.
Implement bounded Bitcoin and PSBT v0 parsing/serialization and P2WPKH SIGHASH_ALL digest construction in the wallet repository, using Bitcoin BIP 143 and BIP 174 fixtures, round-trip tests and malformed/unsupported-input rejection.
Implement testnet/regtest key import, offline public-key/address display and PSBT ownership checks. Keep imported private keys in the signing unikernel only; reject mainnet keys and unsupported address/script/sighash forms in this development version.
Add an app-local encrypted vault design and implementation using established authenticated-encryption and KDF dependencies through existing WASM capabilities, with wrong-passphrase, tamper, lock and restart tests. Persist no plaintext secrets, and disable persistence until these checks pass.
Connect validated transaction review and explicit one-shot approval to signing and signed-PSBT export, with end-to-end regtest fixtures proving outputs, fees, key ownership, signature validity, approval invalidation and no browser/log key disclosure.
Package reproducible build/check/run commands and boot the actual wallet UI as a host-only isolated unikernel. Verify the browser UI and document supported formats, key lifecycle, dependency pins, recovery limitations and the development-only security status.
